    text t_apple "60× farther" below apple gap 16 #8f887c font_size 24 hidden true
    path moon_orbit smooth dashed #8f887c points [[698.4,150],[716.9,185.6],[732.3,222.7],[744.3,260.9],[753,300.1],[758.2,339.9],[760,380],[758.2,420.1],[753,459.9],[744.3,499.1],[732.3,537.3],[716.9,574.4],[698.4,610],[676.8,643.8]] hidden true
    path moon_line dashed #8f887c points [[448,380],[742,380]] style.stroke_width 1.5 hidden true
    text t_60 "60 Earth radii (not to scale)" #8f887c x 595 y 350 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Why the Moon never lands"

Why doesn't the Moon fall onto the Earth? It does fall, all the time; Newton showed why it never lands.
    draw earth mountain

Put a cannon on an impossibly tall mountain and fire a ball sideways, gently: it curves down and lands close by.
    draw short

Fire harder and it lands farther away, and by now the ground has begun to curve away beneath it.
    draw far

At about eight kilometres a second, the ground curves away exactly as fast as the ball falls. It falls forever: an orbit.
    draw orbit

Faster still, past eleven kilometres a second, and it falls too slowly ever to come back.
    draw escape

Every closed orbit lies between these two. The space station's is close to the first, at almost eight kilometres a second.
    focus orbit escape

The Moon is the same ball, sixty Earth radii out, moving sideways at about one kilometre a second.
    draw moon_orbit moon_line t_60 moon sideways l_sideways

In that second it falls just 1.4 millimetres towards us: exactly enough to bend its path round the Earth.
    draw moonpath drop l_drop

Newton checked that number against anything falling here, like an apple, which drops 4.9 metres in its first second.
    shape apple_fruit #b5482e shape circle x 172 y 515 radius 11 style.fill #b5482e
    arrow apple_drop #b5482e from.x 180 from.y 503 to.x 196 to.y 487
    text t_apple_fall "4.9 m in 1 s" #b5482e x 120 y 580 font_size 26

Sixty times farther from the Earth's centre, gravity is sixty squared, 3,600 times weaker.
    draw apple t_apple

And 4.9 metres over 3,600 is 1.4 millimetres. The Moon falls exactly as an apple would, that far away.
    highlight apple part "1.4\\ \\text{mm}"

So the pull that drops the apple is the one that holds the Moon. It never lands only because it keeps moving sideways.
    highlight apple_fruit moon_orbit color #2f6b8f

    quiz "If the Moon stopped moving sideways, what would happen?"
        right: It would fall straight in: gravity pulls on it all the time, and only its sideways speed makes it keep missing.
        + It would fall straight to Earth
        * It would stay where it is
        * It would drift away
---
